135
00:10:02.694 --> 00:10:09.595
(narrator) A classified code file shows the private fields, but the public checkout method returns a PayPalCharge and takes a PayPalAccount.

136
00:10:09.595 --> 00:10:15.373
(narrator) Only these public types are outlined in red, and paper slips with those names slide out of the file: leaked.

137
00:10:15.373 --> 00:10:20.562
(narrator) Private fields get a stamp, not enough; hiding decisions lights up gold beside a sealed envelope.

138
00:10:20.562 --> 00:10:30.137
(narrator) Then a Client case file sends envelopes along an arrow to a gold Contract badge, and a dashed line with a hollow triangle shows that the Server case file implements that contract.

139
00:10:30.137 --> 00:10:39.124
(narrator) Stateless or stateful is typed onto the contract, because callers must know; the database choice, SQL or NoSQL, stays sealed in a top secret envelope inside the server.

140
00:10:39.713 --> 00:10:43.453
♪ “I made every field private. So it’s hidden, right?” ♪

141
00:10:43.903 --> 00:10:46.753
